 The result starts with `Script completed` or `Script failed`, the wall time, and the output. Text and image items appear in order, each on its own line. When the output has more than one text item (from `text()` or `return`), each starts with a `==> text N/M <==` line. `console` calls follow in one `<console_output>` block with one line per call. A failed script keeps its partial output, followed by `Script error:` and the error. Tool calls are real: calls made before a failure are not undone. Calls still running when the script ends are cancelled, and unawaited promises are discarded.
 
+## Worker admission
+
+Each script gets a fresh worker thread and QuickJS VM. Sessions in the same pi process share worker admission, including in-process subagents. Workers start only when scripts need them.
+
+Admission reserves 320 MiB per worker, including the 256 MiB sandbox allowance and 64 MiB of estimated host overhead. Pi keeps one eighth of the process's total memory available as headroom, capped at 1 GiB. The resulting ceiling uses current available memory and counts reservations before workers start. This is an admission budget, not a limit on total process RSS or memory used by host tools.
+
+CPU measurements throttle new starts in 100 ms windows. The initial burst uses available parallelism. Later bursts account for smoothed system CPU utilization. At 85% utilization or above, existing workers finish before another starts. One worker can still make progress when the pool is empty and memory permits. Low-CPU scripts waiting on tools can grow beyond the logical CPU count.
+
+Set `PI_CODEMODE_MAX_WORKERS` to a positive safe integer to impose a lower ceiling. Invalid values fail the call. Changing the ceiling or resource pressure never terminates running scripts. Slots stay reserved until workers actually exit.
+
+A queued session without a worker may bypass the oldest request once. The oldest request then gets priority at the next admission opportunity. Scripts wait at most 60 seconds for admission, or their remaining `timeout_ms`, whichever is shorter. Only a resource-blocked queue needs timed rechecks; ordinary worker exits wake queued scripts directly.
+
